{
  "id": "coast_salish_smokehouse_songs",
  "name": "Coast Salish smokehouse and canoe-journey songs",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Songs of the Coast Salish peoples of the Salish Sea, among them the Stó:lō, Musqueam and Lummi. In the winter spirit dancing (syowen) of the smokehouse, each initiated dancer's personal spirit song is taken up by the assembled drummers and singers, and on the summer Tribal Canoe Journeys, revived with the 1989 Paddle to Seattle, arriving canoes ask permission to land and nations share protocol songs. Pamela Amoss described the winter ceremonial in Coast Salish Spirit Dancing (1978), and the University of Washington Ethnomusicology Archives hold recordings. The hand-drum card stands in for the hide frame drums.",
  "recipe": "classic late-1980s coast salish smokehouse and canoe-journey songs, ritual Devotional, indigenous branch. group vocals, single lead single track no stacking. hand drum source unspecified. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
